# Building a Markov model: states, cycles, transitions and the long tail
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical","b":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical"}
next: {"title":"The ICER, the plane, and why net benefit is easier to think with","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/the-icer-the-plane-and-why-net-benefit.cast"}

chapter: "The states and the cycle"

## Six States for SMA
voice: female
level: advanced
use: markov_model
with:
    title: Nusinersen vs current care — six states, one-year cycles
    states:
      - Pre-symptomatic
      - Not sitting
      - Sitting
      - Walking
      - Permanent ventilation
      - Dead
    transitions:
      - {from: Pre-symptomatic, to: Not sitting, label: '0.60'}
      - {from: Not sitting, to: Sitting, label: '0.28'}
      - {from: Sitting, to: Walking, label: '0.10'}
      - {from: Sitting, to: Not sitting, label: '0.05'}
      - {from: Not sitting, to: Permanent ventilation, label: '0.08'}
      - {from: Permanent ventilation, to: Dead, label: '0.15'}
      - {from: Not sitting, to: Dead, label: '0.06'}
      - {from: Walking, to: Dead, label: '0.01'}
    self_loops:
      - {state: Pre-symptomatic, label: '0.40'}
      - {state: Not sitting, label: '0.58'}
      - {state: Sitting, label: '0.85'}
      - {state: Walking, label: '0.99'}
      - {state: Permanent ventilation, label: '0.85'}
    layout: circle
    highlight_state: Permanent ventilation
sources:
    - id: endear
      title: Nusinersen versus sham control in infantile-onset spinal muscular atrophy (ENDEAR). New England Journal of Medicine 377:1723–1732
      authors: Finkel RS, Mercuri E, Darras BT, et al.
      year: 2017
      finding: 51% of infants on nusinersen versus 0% on sham were motor-milestone responders on HINE-2; the primary endpoint also included event-free survival (time to death or permanent ventilation).

    label note_absorbing "Absorbing: no exit" below attach_to state_dead hidden true

Across six figures we will build one Markov model end to end: its states, its cycle, its transition probabilities, its extrapolated tail, and how you validate a model whose future has not happened yet.
    draw title

The case is nusinersen in spinal muscular atrophy — six injections in the first year, three every year after, for life, at a list price around a hundred and twenty-five thousand dollars each.
    draw state_pre_symptomatic state_label_pre_symptomatic

    pause 0.3

An infant with SMA type 1 never sits unaided; untreated, most are dead or permanently ventilated before their second birthday.
    draw state_not_sitting state_label_not_sitting

In the ENDEAR trial about half the treated infants gained a motor milestone and none of the controls did — so sitting unaided has to be a state the model can actually reach.
    draw state_sitting state_label_sitting

    pause 0.3

Walking is rarer, but real in later-onset patients, and it carries its own utility and its own care costs — that difference is what earns it a separate state.
    draw state_walking state_label_walking

Permanent ventilation sits here for a reason of evidence, not anatomy: it was half of the trial's primary endpoint — death or permanent ventilation.
    draw hl_permanent_ventilation state_permanent_ventilation state_label_permanent_ventilation

And dead: absorbing, with no arrow leaving it. Six states, and every patient must be in exactly one of them at every moment.
    draw state_dead state_label_dead note_absorbing

    pause 0.5

One cycle is one year. Sixty percent of the pre-symptomatic cohort declines into weakness; the remaining forty percent stay — and those two numbers have to exhaust the row.
    draw t_0 t_label_0 loop_pre_symptomatic loop_label_pre_symptomatic

On treatment, a share climb: twenty-eight percent of the not-sitting cohort gains sitting within the year.
    draw t_1 t_label_1

From sitting, ten percent go on to walk and five percent slide back — motor function moves in both directions, and the model must let it.
    draw t_2 t_label_2 t_3 t_label_3

The remaining routes are the grim ones: to ventilation, and from every living state to death.
    draw t_4 t_label_4 t_6 t_label_6 t_5 t_label_5 t_7 t_label_7 parallel true

Whatever is left over each year is staying put — every row of P sums to one, and that arithmetic is exhaustiveness made numerical.
    draw loop_not_sitting loop_label_not_sitting loop_sitting loop_label_sitting loop_walking loop_label_walking loop_permanent_ventilation loop_label_permanent_ventilation parallel true

    pause 0.5

Now the hard test, mutual exclusivity: a ventilated child who can still sit belongs in one state only, so you must rule — ventilation dominates — and write that rule down.
    focus state_sitting state_label_sitting state_permanent_ventilation state_label_permanent_ventilation hl_permanent_ventilation

Clinical honesty is the third test: these milestones come from motor scales like HINE-2, and a milestone is not a utility — each state still needs a u, and in SMA those utilities are thin and mostly proxy-reported by parents.
    focus state_not_sitting state_label_not_sitting state_sitting state_label_sitting state_walking state_label_walking

    pause 0.3

Beslutningsforum said no to nusinersen in 2017 and then yes with an age limit — and an age limit is really a claim about which state patients enter the model in.
    delivery soft

Which brings us to the uncomfortable question. Two children are in sitting: one climbed here from not sitting last year, one is sliding down from walking.
    highlight state_sitting state_label_sitting effect glow

The Markov property forbids the model from telling them apart — the future depends on the state you are in, never on the path that brought you there.
    text note_same_state "One state, two futures" #b5482e x 165 y 195 font_size 25
    mark mark_same_state target note_same_state kind box
    delivery grave

So a state is not a description of a patient; it is the entire memory the model is allowed to keep.
    point at.ref note_same_state gesture underline

    pause 0.4

Everything ahead is repair work on that lost memory: the half-cycle correction, tunnel states, time-dependent transitions, and the extrapolated tail where nusinersen's whole result actually lives.

Next figure: one year is a long time in an infant's motor development — so we ask what the cycle length is doing to these six states, and what its half is for.

## The Cycle and Its Half
voice: female
level: advanced
use: survival_curve
with:
    time_label: Years (1 cycle = 1 year)
    show_median: false
    title: Half-cycle correction
    arms:
      - {label: Counted at cycle start, color: demand, survival: [1, 0.88, 0.774, 0.681, 0.6, 0.528, 0.464]}
      - {label: Counted at cycle end, color: supply, survival: [0.88, 0.774, 0.681, 0.6, 0.528, 0.464, 0.408]}
      - {label: Half-cycle corrected, color: accent, survival: [0.94, 0.827, 0.728, 0.641, 0.564, 0.496, 0.436]}
sources:
    - id: barendregt2009
      title: 'The half-cycle correction: banish rather than explain it. Medical Decision Making 29(4):500–502'
      authors: Barendregt JJ
      year: 2009
      finding: Half-cycle correction is an approximation to integrating the Markov trace; a proper life-table (trapezoid) calculation makes it unnecessary.

Six states for SMA still need a clock — and this part is about the half cycle that clock keeps losing.
    draw axes

One cycle is one year, per our notation, so the model only ever looks at the cohort on the anniversaries: a thousand children on nusinersen, then 880, then 774, at a modelled twelve percent annual mortality.
    draw arm_0 arm_label_0

Between two anniversaries the trace is flat, and then it falls off a cliff — the cohort is frozen for a year, then updated all at once.
    point at.ref arm_0 gesture underline

Cycle length is a real modelling choice: short enough that at most one clinically meaningful event happens inside it, long enough that your data, your costs and the four percent discount rate arrive at that frequency.
    highlight arm_0 effect glow

But children do not die on their birthdays: deaths are spread through the year, and this trace credits every one of them with a full extra year of life.
    focus arm_0

    pause 0.4

Count at the end of each cycle instead — only the 880 survivors are credited with year one — and the staircase drops to the opposite extreme.
    draw arm_1 arm_label_1

Same cohort, same transition matrix; the only thing that changed is where inside the cycle you hold the ruler.
    point at.ref arm_1 gesture circle

Sum the upper trace over six years and each child accrues 4.46 life-years.
    highlight arm_0 effect glow

Sum the lower one and the very same child accrues 3.93.
    highlight arm_1 effect glow

That gap is 0.54 life-years — over a tenth of the total — and it has a closed form: the starting proportion minus the ending proportion, times one cycle.
    delivery grave

    pause 0.4

Half-cycle correction is the compromise between them: average the start and the end of every cycle, which is exactly the trapezoid rule under the survival curve.
    draw arm_2 arm_label_2

Its six-year total is 4.195 life-years; the exact integral of the underlying exponential is 4.190 — accurate to within about two days.
    focus arm_2

So it isn't a fudge factor — it is the claim that events fall mid-cycle on average, so a cohort spends half a cycle in the state it is leaving.

The same half-cycle shift belongs on costs and on discounting: apply the discount factor to the middle of the cycle, not to its start.
    highlight arm_2 effect pulse

    pause 0.3

Two moves make this whole argument shrink.
    clear keep ["axes","arm_2","arm_label_2"]

First, shorten the cycle: the error scales with cycle length, so a monthly cycle turns that 0.54 life-year disagreement into roughly 0.04.
    highlight arm_2 effect glow

Second — Jan Barendregt's argument from 2009 — banish the correction rather than explain it, and integrate the trace properly with a life-table calculation.
    show arm_0 arm_label_0 arm_1 arm_label_1

And here is what catches people out: across two arms the half-cycle errors largely cancel, so plenty of ICERs barely move when you apply it.
    focus arm_0 arm_1

They cancel only when both curves fall at the same pace — and nusinersen against natural history is precisely the case where they don't: the untreated cohort loses most of its children in the first two cycles, where the correction bites hardest.
    point at.ref arm_0 gesture circle delivery grave

One year is the cycle; half a year is what the model owes every child who dies inside it — and in SMA almost all of that half year lands on the comparator's side of the ICER.
    draw title

